1. One counterexample: a bug where the implementation does not update accounting statistics when freeing blocks, but for the first transaction (mkfs and writing one small file), no blocks are freed so the bug does not trigger. Trace: during mkfs, allocate 10 blocks for system structures; during file write, allocate one data unit; no frees occur. Accounting statistics show 11 allocated blocks. Checker verifies allocated statistics equal traversal sum from disk image, both 11, so green. Recovery and record verifier also pass for normal run. Self-tests for C22 and C80 pass when run with reuse or no flush, as they are separate tests.

2. For a 32 KiB data unit with parent pointer and physical block size 4 KiB, any torn state (some blocks new, some old) will have a checksum that does not match the parent pointer's stored checksum, because the checksum covers the entire unit. Thus, there is no torn state that the parent checksum would accept, and the collapse to not durable is sound.

3. All invariants listed as not applicable (I-3.4 to I-3.7, I-4.4, I-4.6, I-4.7, I-6) are correctly not judgeable for the first transaction due to missing features (no snapshots, intents, encryption). All invariants in the judgeable list (I-1.1 to I-1.5, I-2.1 to I-2.3, I-3.1, I-5.1, I-5.2, I-7.1 to I-7.6, I-8.1 to I-8.5, I-4.1, I-4.2, I-4.3, I-4.8) can be judged with the bytes defined in F6 and F7 for the first transaction image.

4. The counterexample of the block freeing bug is reachable as a real implementation error that escapes detection in the first transaction tests. Running a crash-replay test that includes block freeing operations would immediately expose this bug by causing the accounting to be inconsistent during reuse, changing the answer to show that the bug is caught.
